---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import home2019 from "../../assets/images/case-studies/umbc-website/homepage-2019.png";
import home2026 from "../../assets/images/case-studies/umbc-website/homepage-2026.png";
---

<Base
  title="UMBC.edu Rebuild"
  description="Rebuilt UMBC.edu from a PHP link farm into a unified WordPress platform on Pantheon, migrating 19 legacy sites."
>
  <section class="pt-32 pb-20 md:pt-40">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
      <a
        href="/case-studies/"
        class="text-sm text-muted hover:text-white transition-colors"
      >
        &larr; Case Studies
      </a>

      <h1 class="text-6xl md:text-8xl mt-6 mb-4">
        UMBC.edu Rebuild
      </h1>
      <span class="badge">Platform</span>

      <div class="mt-12 space-y-12">
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
          <figure class="panel p-3">
            <Image src={home2019} alt="The UMBC.edu homepage in 2019: a photo slideshow above tabs for different audiences, each holding columns of plain links out to other sites." class="w-full h-auto rounded-sm" />
            <figcaption class="text-muted text-sm mt-3 px-1">2019: one PHP page, mostly links out to other sites.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="panel p-3">
            <Image src={home2026} alt="The UMBC.edu homepage today: a full-width hero promoting fall events, with navigation for About, Academics, Admissions and Aid, Research, Life on Campus, and Alumni and Giving." class="w-full h-auto rounded-sm" />
            <figcaption class="text-muted text-sm mt-3 px-1">Today: a unified WordPress platform with 7,000+ posts and 300+ pages.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <!-- Problem -->
        <div class="panel p-6 md:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl mb-4">The Problem</h2>
          <p class="text-white/80 leading-relaxed">
            UMBC.edu was a single PHP homepage that functioned as a
            link farm, pointing visitors out to hundreds of scattered
            subdomains and microsites, some dating back to the 1990s.
            Content was fragmented across 550+ subdomains with no
            consistent design, no shared navigation, and no central
            content management. Departments had no easy way to keep
            their web presence current. The result was a digital
            experience that didn't reflect the university's quality.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- What I Built -->
        <div class="panel p-6 md:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl mb-4">What I Built</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4 text-white/80 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              I rebuilt UMBC.edu as a unified WordPress platform on
              Pantheon with a custom theme and custom plugins that I
              developed. The project involved:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 ml-4">
              <li class="pl-4 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.6em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:bg-accent-ink">
                Migrating 19 legacy sites into the new platform
              </li>
              <li class="pl-4 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.6em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:bg-accent-ink">
                Consolidating 7,000+ posts and 300+ pages into a
                coherent content architecture
              </li>
              <li class="pl-4 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.6em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:bg-accent-ink">
                Auditing 550 scattered subdomains with the Library and
                DoIT, archiving SEO-rich legacy content with
                rank-preserving redirects
              </li>
              <li class="pl-4 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.6em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:bg-accent-ink">
                Co-managing, with IT, the separate WordPress Multisite
                that runs nearly 600 department sites, where departments
                own their own content
              </li>
              <li class="pl-4 relative before:content-[''] before:absolute before:left-0 before:top-[0.6em] before:w-1.5 before:h-1.5 before:bg-accent-ink">
                Designing a plain-language taxonomy ("Area of Study")
                that allows multi-category assignments across content
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Results -->
        <div class="panel p-6 md:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl mb-4">Results</h2>
          <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-6">
            <div class="text-center">
              <p class="stat text-5xl">7,000+</p>
              <p class="text-muted text-sm mt-1">Posts migrated</p>
            </div>
            <div class="text-center">
              <p class="stat text-5xl">300+</p>
              <p class="text-muted text-sm mt-1">Pages</p>
            </div>
            <div class="text-center">
              <p class="stat text-5xl">~600</p>
              <p class="text-muted text-sm mt-1">Department sites on the Multisite</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="text-white/80 leading-relaxed">
            The strategy was to build out UMBC.edu so it answers about 90%
            of what our audiences come looking for, and send the other 10%
            to department sites. The platform replaced a fragmented web
            presence with a single, managed system. Departments can maintain their own
            content within a consistent design and navigation framework.
            Nineteen legacy sites were consolidated, with rank-preserving
            redirects for legacy content.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
